---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";

import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import banner from "../assets/img/core-proxies-banner.jpg";
import osm from "../assets/img/arney-osm26.jpg";
import PS128 from "../assets/img/arney-deck-monkey_llj.jpg";
import tenby from "../assets/img/arney-tenby-2022_md.jpg";

const descr =
  "Tom Arney is a geochemist at the University of Southampton using laser ablation mass spectrometry to generate high-resolution records of environmental proxies.";

const collabs = [
  "University of Southampton",
  "British Antarctic Survey",
  "Heidelberg University",
  "University College Dublin",
  "Alfred Wegener Institut",
  "Toyama University",
  "University of Portsmouth",
  "Colorado College",
  "University of Cambridge",
  "British Ocean Sediment Core Research Facility",
];
---

<Page title="Tom Arney" description={descr}>
  {/* <Figure src={banner} alt="" /> */}

  <h1>Microanalytical geochemist</h1>

  <p class="mb-2">
    I use high-resolution geochemical methods to work on problems across
    geology, palaeoclimate and environmental science.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-8 sm:flex-row mt-8">
    <div class="shrink-0">
      <Image
        src={osm}
        class="not-prose sm:mt-2 mx-auto"
        height="350"
        alt="a photo of Tom Arney presenting a poster at a conference"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="md:mt-2">
      <p class="mt-0">
        Both physical and biological processes alter the chemical or isotopic
        composition of natural materials, typically in response to the ambient
        conditions at the time of formation &ndash; such as the high
        temperatures and pressures <a
          href="/research/west-antarctic-geology-from-fsp-pb-rb-sr/"
          >as a feldspar grain crystallises</a
        >, or the temperature, salinity and pH of sea water <a
          href="research/ggrip-2026-optimising-laser-methods-for-long-sst-from-corals/"
          >as a coral grows</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        These compositional variations can be used to reconstruct past
        environmental conditions, or to understand the processes that have
        shaped the Earth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Many of these properties are particularly interesting at the micro scale,
    and laser ablation&ndash;based methods enable analysis at the scale of tens
    of microns.
  </p>

  {
    /*    
    <div class="mt-28">
        <h2>Collaborations</h2>

        <div class="flex gap-y-1 gap-x-6 flex-wrap justify-center text-center">
            {collabs.map((i) => <span>{i}</span>)}
        </div>
    </div> 
*/
  }

  <h2 class="mt-20">Methods & analytical experience</h2>
  <p>
    I primarily use <b>laser-ablation&ndash;based techniques</b> for high-resolution
    geochemistry, including major and trace element analysis in-situ isotopic systems,
    and geochronology.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also have experience with scanning electron microscopy, sample preparation
    for microanalysis, and developing <a
      href="https://github.com/tomarney/repeated-ratio-regressions-DRS"
      >software</a
    > for data processing and <a
      href="https://github.com/tomarney/coupled-Pb-Rb-Sr-inverse-provenance"
      >reproducible research</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    More details on my <a href="/research/#methods">research</a> page.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-8 sm:flex-row mt-20">
    <a href="posts/polar-post" class="sm:order-2 shrink-0">
      <Image
        src={PS128}
        class="not-prose mx-auto mt-2"
        height="400"
        alt="a photo of a man in bright orange waterproofs on the working deck of a ship in sea ice"
      />
    </a>

    <div>
      <h2 class="mt-0">Antarctic expedition</h2>
      <p>
        The highlight of my scientific adventures so far was sailing as a
        micropalaeontologist on a marine geology expedition on the German
        research icebreaker <i>Polarstern</i>. Read a series of letters I wrote
        documenting the two months I spent on the East Antarctic coast <a
          href="posts/polar-post">here</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Photo shows me engaged in the glamorous job of "deck monkey", clearing
        mud off the deck (though with a nice view).
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-8 sm:flex-row mt-20">
    <div class="shrink-0">
      <Image
        src={tenby}
        class="not-prose mx-auto mt-2"
        height="400"
        alt="a man in a red hard hat pointing at some rocks, explaining something"
      />
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="mt-0">Teaching & outreach</h2>
      <p>
        I've helped deliver practical sessions covering palaeoceanography, ocean
        biogeochemistry, geological mapping, statistics, spectral analysis, R
        and MATLAB, and field courses (my favourite) on the Jurassic Coast and
        in Pembrokeshire.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've also talked about ocean temperature at the Southampton Science and
        Engineering Festival, about corals at the <a
          href="https://britishsciencefestival.org/">British Science Festival</a
        >, and given a talk on my Antarctic research at the <a
          href="https://pintofscience.co.uk/event/global-scale"
          >Pint of Science</a
        > festival in Southampton.
        {
          /* https://web.archive.org/web/20250821143056/https://pintofscience.co.uk/event/global-scale */
        }
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Page>
